## Why This Is Asked

Tests lock-free IPC design, precise memory ordering, and verification under contention.

## Key Concepts

- lock-free primitives, per-slot sequencing, memory barriers, mmap-based IPC, cache coherence.
- detection of partial writes and ensuring visibility under contention.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t seq; char payload[256]; } slot_t;

void write_slot(slot_t *s, void *data, size_t n){
  __atomic_store_n(&s->seq, 0, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  memcpy(s->payload, data, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&s->seq, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

bool read_slot(slot_t *s, void *out){
  uint64_t a = __atomic_load_n(&s->seq, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  if (a != 1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, 256);
  uint64_t b = __atomic_load_n(&s->seq, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  return (a == b && a == 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of concurrency in OS caches, race handling, and data integrity under contention.

## Key Concepts
- CAS-based locking and per-block loading state
- Wait queues and wakeups for concurrent readers
- Read-ahead decisions and eviction interactions

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch in C-like pseudo
typedef struct BlockEntry { inode_t *inode; int block_no; void *data; atomic_int loading; atomic_int ready; wait_queue_t wq; } BlockEntry;
BlockEntry *cache_lookup(inode_t*, int); void disk_read_async(inode_t*, int, void*);

void* get_block(inode_t *inode, int block_no) {
  BlockEntry *e = cache_lookup(inode, block_no);
  if (e && atomic_load(&e->ready)) return e->data;
  if (e == NULL) {
    e = allocate_entry(inode, block_no);
    if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&e->loading, 0, 1)) {
      disk_read_async(inode, block_no, e->data);
      atomic_store(&e->ready, 1);
      wake_all(&e->wq);
    } else {
      wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
    }
  } else {
    wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
  }
  return e->data;
}
```

